course: dictionary
lesson: 12358
title: One word, two meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one the writer means. The word criminally can mean breaking the law, or it can mean shockingly bad, as if it should be a crime. «The man was charged criminally after the robbery.» The clues are charged and robbery, so this is about the law.
explain: «That band is criminally underrated!» There is no crime here. The speaker is joking: the band is far too little known, so criminally means very badly, as if it should be a crime. Ask yourself: is someone breaking a rule, or is something simply unfair or shocking?
explain: To check your meaning, swap it into the sentence. "In a way that breaks the law" fits «charged criminally», but it does not make sense in «criminally underrated». When one meaning does not fit, try the other.
fact: Criminally means in a way that breaks the law and is a crime.
fact: «Criminally responsible» means the law says a person is to blame for a crime.
fact: In informal talk, criminally means shockingly or very badly, as if it should be a crime.
fact: «Criminally underrated» means a person or thing is far too little known or praised.
fact: The news and the law use criminally in its first meaning; friends joking about a song or a film often use the second.
exercise: «The shop owner was charged criminally after selling stolen goods.» What does criminally mean here? | Very quickly | In a kind way | With a big smile | *In a way that breaks the law
exercise: «That singer is criminally underrated, so listen to her songs.» What does criminally mean here? | Very loudly | *Shockingly, as if it should be a crime | Only just | Legally, with a judge in the room
exercise: Which phrase uses criminally in the legal sense, about breaking the law? | Criminally cute | *Criminally responsible | Criminally funny | Criminally underrated
test: «The film was criminally ignored at the awards.» What does criminally suggest? | It was against the law to show | *It was shockingly unfair, far too little praised | It was very short | The film was a crime to watch
test: Ella says «The bike was criminally cheap» means the bike was against the law. What should she check? | *Whether the meaning fits: cheap is not a crime, so it means shockingly | Whether the bike is red | Whether it is in a newspaper | Whether a judge is in the sentence
exam: Which sentence uses criminally in the joking sense? | The thief was caught criminally | The driver was charged criminally | He was criminally responsible for the fire | *The tickets were criminally expensive
